WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our first guest tonight
is a musical legend,

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:05.938
a 20-time Grammy Award winner,
an Academy Award winner,

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:08.107
a Tony Award winner,
and member of both

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:10.743
the Songwriters Hall of Fame and
the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:13.713
His new album,
"Only the Strong Survive,"

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:14.780
is available now.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.049
Please welcome to the show,
The Boss,

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.085
Bruce Springsteen, everybody!

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.087
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:31.097 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:41.107 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:45.011 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:46.980
Welcome to the show.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.149
I'm so happy to have you here.
-Thank you.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.550
Glad to be here.
-Last time --

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.921
Last time I saw you was
backstage at your Broadway show,

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.589
which was fantastic.
Was that something --

00:00:57.589 --> 00:00:59.792
Looking back at it,
can you believe now you did it?

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:02.027
Was that ever something you
thought you were gonna do?

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:08.735
-We did 236 of them,
so, uh, it took 14 months.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.504
About five days of --
five nights a week.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.774
And I loved every night of it,
you know?

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.108
It wasn't something
I thought I'd do

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:17.944
'cause it happened by accident.

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.813
You know, I was --

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:24.550
Two weeks before President Obama
was leaving the White House,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.819
he asked me
to come down and play.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:29.421
So I said, "Well, I don't
want to bring the band.

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:32.558
Too much noise."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.827
"I'll go down by myself
with my acoustic guitar,

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:39.332
and I'll read something from my
book, and I'll sing a song."

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:43.236
So that's -- I spent a couple
hours in my studio,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.371
and I worked up about
90 minutes of something.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.907
I went down, and I played
in the East Room

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.143
to just a couple hundred people.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:54.046
But it was basically 90 minutes
of the Broadway play, you know?

00:01:54.046 --> 00:01:56.248
And so he came up to me
afterwards, and he said,

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:58.952
"Gee, you know,
I'm glad you did that for us,

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.319
but that should be a show."

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:04.624
So I said, "It's good
presidential advice, you know?"

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.225
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:10.095
-And is it true he takes a cut?
He took 15%?

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:11.931
-Unfortunately, it was true.
That's true.

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:13.066
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:14.133
-I don't know
if you remember this,

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.301
but we were lucky enough --

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.368
I brought my mom
to see the show,

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:17.837
and we went backstage,

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:20.173
and you were being very gracious
with your time.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:22.909
And I was very cognizant
of the fact that

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:26.211
you're a busy guy,
and, wonderfully,

00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:28.581
Patty stepped in and said,
"Before they leave, Bruce,

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:30.817
you got to take a picture
with Seth's mom,"

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:34.120
and this photo is celebrated
in our house.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.388
[ Laughter, audience "Awws" ]

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.390
-She looks great.
-She looks wonderful.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.857
Doesn't she look good?
-She looks fabulous.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:41.160
[ Applause ]
[ Chuckles ]

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.230
-The thing -- the joke
my brother and I always say is,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.633
we've never seen he look that
happy in a picture with our dad.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:49.669
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.205
I've never seen a photo of her
where her eyes aren't rolling.

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:55.942
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:57.943
This is --
This album, these are,

00:02:57.943 --> 00:03:00.913
obviously, soul songs
that mean something to you.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:02.848
-Yeah. It was just a lot
of stuff that I liked.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:07.253
And it was during the lockdown,
so I'd made a couple of records.

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.654
Made a record
called "Western Stars,"

00:03:08.654 --> 00:03:09.989
and I made a film with it.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.423
Made a record called
"Letter to You"

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:13.926
with The E Street Band,
and I made a film with that.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.627
'Cause you couldn't
go out on the road.

00:03:15.627 --> 00:03:20.667
So, still sitting there
with no schedule in front of me,

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.735
so I said, "Well, I'd like to --

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.137
Let me do something
I've never done before.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:26.339
Let me do something
that's centered on my voice

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:27.673
and just singing."

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:31.578
The greatest vocal music,
in my opinion, of all time

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:34.413
is gospel music
and then soul music.

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:36.482
All the singers
came from the church.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:41.119
So I just dug into my collection
of my favorite singers.

00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:43.822
Jerry Butler and David Ruffin
from The Temptations,

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:48.327
Levi Stubbs from The Four Tops,
William Bell.

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:53.665
And I just started to go through
music that I found that

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:57.436
I had a good range for
and fit my voice pretty well.

00:03:57.436 --> 00:04:01.574
And I made one record,
I threw it out.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:05.178
And then, I finally
focused on the soul --

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:08.647
the idea of making
a cohesive soul-music record.

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:10.783
And so that's what we did.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.084
I really -- I did it for myself.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:13.719
It was a lot of fun.
I hope people enjoy it.

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.254
-You know, there's a great --

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.090
Sam Moore, from Sam &amp; Dave,
is on a couple of the songs.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.492
I know you've sung with him
in the past.

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:22.361
-Yeah.
-On stage, in concert.

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:25.264
You know, you've sort of given
this gift to people.

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:26.766
You know, you did a song
with Jack Antonov.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.033
Obviously,
you mean a lot to him.

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.469
He gets to sing with you.

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.304
What's it like for you when
you get to sing with somebody

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:33.840
who obviously meant a lot to you
in your early days?

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.979
-Well, Sam &amp; Dave were gigantic
in my musical development,

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:42.348
'cause what most people
don't understand,

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.785
Sam Moore
was an incredible band leader.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:48.954
And I used to go see Sam &amp; Dave
when they played

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:52.425
The Satellite Lounge
in Fort Dix, New Jersey,

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:54.560
The Fast Lane in Asbury Park.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.963
These are 200-, 300-seat clubs.

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:01.601
And this was in the late '70s.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:03.703
They were not --

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.439
I don't know if Sam was speaking
to Dave at the time.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:07.740
They weren't getting along,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.546
but they were still incredible,
you know?

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.648
So incredible that, one night,
I stood in the back

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:19.285
of a tiny club
that was only half full.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:22.788
And I knew I was seeing
some of the greatest music

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:25.825
in the United States
that very moment.

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:27.493
And I just cried, you know?

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.595
That --
Just over how good they were.

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:31.297
Just 'cause they were so good.

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:33.833
And so I've known Sam
for about 30 years.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.968
And we've sung together
on a few things.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:39.739
And he's a great guy,
and probably

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:42.341
our greatest living
soul singer right now.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.977
-And is it a quick "yes"
when you call up Sam and say,

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:46.812
"Do you want to be
on the new album?"

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:49.715
-Yeah, Sam's game to sing.
He still loves singing.

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.417
He's 86,
but still loves to sing.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:53.686
-You can't -- You wouldn't guess
86 when you listen to it.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:56.689
-No, no, he's got -- his voice
has sustained beautifully.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:57.824
So he's wonderful.

00:05:57.824 --> 00:05:59.659
-And you're going back
on tour, as well?

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:01.461
-E Street Band.
-E Street Band.

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:03.629
-Coming back on the road.
-Unbelievable.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:06.265
-February 1st.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:09.168
Thank you.
-Now are you --

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.303
You know, I'm always --
every time I've been

00:06:11.303 --> 00:06:13.339
lucky enough to see you live,
you know, I cannot believe

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.740
the energy
that goes into that show,

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.375
not just from you but
from the entire E Street Band.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:18.210
When you have February 1st
in the future,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.678
are you already
thinking about it?

00:06:19.678 --> 00:06:21.613
Are you thinking like,
"We gotta get into show shape

00:06:21.613 --> 00:06:23.749
right now"?
-Yeah.

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:26.085
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:27.686
-It's, uh...

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:32.859
The thing with show shape is,
almost no matter what you do,

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:35.494
you are still going to wake up
the next morning

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:37.229
after that first show

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:39.498
feeling like you've been
beaten with a baseball bat.

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:40.899
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:44.837
Every single thing
is going to hurt you.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:48.307
Every muscle and bone
in your body,

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:52.244
your voice is going to feel like
it will never sing again.

00:06:52.244 --> 00:06:54.646
And then you play
the second night,

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:55.948
"Eh, it's not so bad."

00:06:55.948 --> 00:06:58.050
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.052
-I got think you got to just
stick with it, right?

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.754
-Yeah, by the time
you're three or four nights in,

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:05.257
your body adapts,
and you get used to

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:08.194
being constantly tired
because you are.

00:07:08.194 --> 00:07:12.064
And so I live with fatigue
for about a year and a half.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:15.401
Your immune system drops,
so you gotta get used to getting

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:18.871
a little more regularly sick
just because of the --

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:21.273
to the degree that
you're pushing your body.

00:07:21.273 --> 00:07:26.145
But it's still the greatest
[bleep] thing in the world.

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:29.448
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:32.418
-I mean...

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:34.353
Obviously, it sounds like
you stayed busy

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.488
and you realized, you know,
there was an opportunity

00:07:36.488 --> 00:07:37.789
with the pandemic
to do other things.

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:40.560
But I imagine from you,
the entire E Street Band,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.795
from top to bottom, everybody
must be just so excited to be --

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:44.030
-Well, it's been --
we've been six years.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.898
So it's a long time.
-Yeah.

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:49.635
-And, you know, I mean,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:52.505
it takes us two seconds
to come back with each other.

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:54.706
But, yes, it's been --
we've been off for a long time,

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:56.308
so looking forward to it.

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:58.277
-We're gonna take a quick
commercial break, but I --

00:07:58.277 --> 00:07:59.345
You don't know this, either,

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:00.746
but I do want
to tell you something.

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:04.516
One more thing about my mom,
which is we -- our first --

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:06.585
Not our first dance, but the
mother/son dance at our wedding,

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.287
she always wanted to do --

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:09.688
she wanted us to dance
to a Bruce Springsteen song.

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:10.989
-All right.
-And so the band

00:08:10.989 --> 00:08:14.427
had to learn it,
and the song was "For You,"

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:17.262
from "Asbury" -- you know,
from your first album.

00:08:17.262 --> 00:08:20.899
And my wife pointed out,
"That's a crazy song

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:23.636
that your mom wanted you
to sing a song where the lyrics

00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:26.705
are, 'I came for you, for you.'"

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:28.741
She's like, "Why would you
do that on a day

00:08:28.741 --> 00:08:30.643
where you're seriously here
for me?"

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:33.779
[ Laughter ]

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:35.714
She goes, "I think this is
a very aggressive song

00:08:35.714 --> 00:08:38.717
for your mom to have picked."
-Now, get this.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:40.353
I'm the guy that wrote it.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:43.155
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:47.226
So I know exactly what you're
talking about, you know?

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:48.461
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:51.397
It's got -- you know,
it's a strange song.

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:52.898
I don't know what it means.

00:08:52.898 --> 00:08:54.700
[ Laughter ]

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.237
It sounds suicidal to me.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:02.208
But -- And it's a very unusual
song for the wedding...

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:03.776
-Yeah.
-...and the son/mother dance.

00:09:03.776 --> 00:09:05.478
But, God bless.
-You know what?

00:09:05.478 --> 00:09:06.678
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.678 --> 00:09:08.147
She thinks outside the box,
Bruce.

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:09.515
I don't know what to tell you.

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:11.349
I got more to ask.
We'll be right back

00:09:11.349 --> 00:09:13.185
with more
from Bruce Springsteen.

